  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 104 PM EDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nEarlier this morning, some lake effect clouds formed off Lake \nHuron and resulted in partly cloudy skies over northern MI. \nThis morning's 12z KAPX RAOB depicts nice lapse rates around 3 \nkft and below, with some breezy east winds right off the surface\nin this layer (above the shallow morning surface inversion). \nThese ideal lapse rates are halted around 3 to 4 kft as a fairly\nthick warm and dry layer exists up to the mid levels. Earlier satellite\nrevealed the prominence of this inversion with cumulus becoming\nspread out and eventually dissipating as the clouds track east \nto west over northern MI. This cloud cover kept overnight \ntemperatures in the high 30s to mid 40s in northern lower. A few\ncold pockets of low to high 30s were seen in typical cold \nspots. Widespread 30 to 35 degree temperatures were seen over \nthe eastern U.P. where less cloud cover and even light drainage \nwinds off the CAN shield were seen last night. This afternoon,\nthe boundary layer is slightly rising and mixing into that\ndry inversion layer resulting in a gradual dissipation of cloud\ncover over northern lower. \n\nAbove our heads, an upper ridge is taking shape on the northern side \nof a weakening closed upper low over the northern plains. This rex \nblock will become established over MI as it creeps east tonight \nand into Wednesday. This feature will help anchor the surface \nhigh pressure to the northeast, keeping northern MI influenced \nby this drier airmass through the weekend. Although the airmass \nremains dry, warmer temperatures will slowly work there way in \neach day as this airmass modifies and warmer air aloft sinks \ncloser to the surface. Northeast winds will veer southeast this\nevening, remaining breezy near the Lake huron coast. High swim \nrisk exists through late this evening for the sunrise side \nbeaches due to gusty winds and waves of 4 ft. \n\nAs the core of the surface high shift to the east tonight before\nanchoring for the next several days, temperatures aloft will \nslightly warm with winds remaining breezy near the coast. Near \nfreezing temperatures continue to be forecasted for eastern \nupper due to ideal flow off the CAN shield, however slightly \nwarmer conditions (high 30s to high 40s) are expected over \nnorthern lower tonight. Isolated pockets of low to mid 30s \nremain likely tonight for cold spots, however the surface \ninversion should continue to remain shallow and only allow for a\nfew hours of temperatures at their coolest near sunrise. \n\nBesides gradual warming each day, occasionally breezy southeast \nwinds and dry conditions will continue into the weekend. Slight \nchances for rain return near the end of the forecast period (mid to \nlate next week), however we have been teased on day 7 in fall\nbefore.",
